  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.043,
            0.848,
            0.059
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell: The Building Block of Life"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 23"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.088,
            0.676,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.5 Cell Theory — The Unifying Principle of Biology"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.111,
            0.686,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "An important observation about living organisms is that all organisms are made up of cells. In 1838, a German botanist named Matthias Schleiden reported that all plants are made up of cells. In 1839, German zoologist Theodor Schwann, found that all animals are also made up of cells. Later, in 1855, a German scientist named Rudolf Virchow, further expanded the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell Theory",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " by stating that new cells are formed only from pre-existing cells. Together, their work led to the formulation of the Cell Theory."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.267,
            0.481,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "According to the classical Cell Theory:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.287,
            0.645,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y All living organisms are made up of one or more cells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.308,
            0.685,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y The cell is the basic unit of structure and function in living beings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.345,
            0.492,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y All cells arise from pre-existing cells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.366,
            0.685,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This unifies all biology, from bacteria to humans, and explains life’s continuity through cell division."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.41,
            0.59,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.5.1 Do cells grow and reproduce forever?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.432,
            0.686,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cells grow and divide in a controlled way, stay in the right place, carry out their functions, and eventually die when they are no longer needed. Dead cells are replaced by new cells that carry out the same function. Thus, every cell has a definite life span. If cells do not die when they should or if they die too early problems can arise in the body. In many animal cells, cell division usually stops when cells come in contact with neighbouring cells. This process is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "contact inhibition",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". However, cancer cells lose this control and keep dividing uncontrollably, leading to the formation of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "tumours",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Plant cells grow differently. Due to their rigid cell walls plant cells do not show contact inhibition and follow a different pattern of growth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.637,
            0.686,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Even though cells are extremely tiny they perform remarkable functions. They produce energy, synthesise and secrete substances, divide to form new cells, and work together to maintain the proper functioning of the body. Every living being from a tiny bacterium to a giant tree is made up of cells. You are one of the products of these tiny hardworking cells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.077,
            0.743,
            0.133,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.077,
                0.743,
                0.133,
                0.8
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.754,
            0.358,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.782,
            0.585,
            0.799
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How do cells monitor their growth to maintain a balance?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.801,
            0.664,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cells also have natural ways of dying to maintain a balance. Programmed Cell Death (PCD) is a genetically regulated and organised process of selective cell destruction. It is essential for normal development, cellular quality control and immune function. When an embryo develops, the PCD helps form fingers by eliminating cells between digits, without it we would have webbed hands."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.897,
            0.627,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Explore different ways by which cells maintain themselves."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.726,
            0.086,
            0.893,
            0.102
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.725,
            0.122,
            0.827,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 18,
              "bbox": [
                0.725,
                0.122,
                0.827,
                0.214
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_18.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.836,
            0.117,
            0.944,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In 1902, the Austrian botanist, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Gottlieb Haberlandt",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", proposed that "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "any",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.72,
            0.225,
            0.94,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "living plant cell",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", even a fully mature cell from a permanent tissue, can develop into a complete plant if it is provided with suitable nutrients and favourable conditions. He suggested that plant cells have the ability to form different types of cells and also change them. This special ability of plant cells is called totipotency. Haberlandt’s idea laid the foundation for a new branch of biology known as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Plant Tissue Culture Technology.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.696,
            0.556,
            0.773,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 21,
              "bbox": [
                0.696,
                0.556,
                0.773,
                0.6
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_21.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.779,
            0.557,
            0.897,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.723,
            0.603,
            0.935,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How do cancer cells grow and spread?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.722,
            0.637,
            0.931,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Normal cells grow, age and die in a controlled manner. Sometimes, this system breaks down, and abnormal cells start growing and dividing uncontrollably. This results in the formation of tumours, which may be benign or malignant. Cancerous tumours can invade nearby tissues and even spread to other parts of the body to form new tumours."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
